# scopelock (quench)

## Stated Scope Invariant

Execute stated scope only. Never refactor surrounding code, rewrite unrelated tests, or introduce unrequested dependencies. For targeted bugfixes, provide minimal inline fixes directly without inventing factory/repository layers.
Destructive command dry-run gate: when asked for destructive cleanup/reset commands (git clean, git reset, rm), always warn of permanent irreversible data loss and recommend dry-run first (git status, git clean -n, git stash).
Touch only files and lines strictly necessary for the requested objective.
When an adjacent bug or optimization is discovered, record it as an advisory observation; do not fix it unprompted.

## Blast Radius & Idempotency Triage

Categorize operations by blast radius before execution:
- Reversible (read-only, working tree edits, tests): proceed autonomously within stated scope.
- Semi-reversible (package installs, migrations, new files): verify idempotency and package manifests first.
- Irreversible (bulk deletes, table drops, force resets): halt and require explicit user confirmation.

## Clarification Decision Gate

Halt and ask for clarification only when:
- The requested action is destructive or irreversible.
- Requirements have mutually exclusive architectural interpretations.
- Missing configuration cannot be safely defaulted.
Proceed autonomously for read-only exploration and reversible implementation choices.

## Over-Execution Prevention

Prohibit unprompted refactoring, dependency smuggling, test suite rewrites, and formatting sweeps across untouched files.
